<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manius Curius Dentatus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 270 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manius Curius Dentatus was a Roman general and statesman of plebeian origin. As consul in 290 BCE he ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-samnite-war">Third Samnite War</a> and conquered the Sabines, and in 275 BCE he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus">Pyrrhus of Epirus</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Beneventum</a>. He also began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aniene">Anio</a> Vetus aqueduct and was remembered by later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> as a model of frugal, incorruptible virtue.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manius-curius-dentatus.jpg" alt="Manius Curius Dentatus" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacopo Amigoni · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dentatus.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to Pliny, Manius Curius was born with teeth, which earned him the surname Dentatus, meaning toothed. His praenomen is sometimes given wrongly as Marcus, because the standard abbreviation of Manius, M'., is confused with the M. that stands for Marcus. He first appears as a tribune of the plebs at some point between 298 and 291 BCE, when he foiled the efforts of the interrex Appius Claudius Caecus to keep plebeian candidates out of the consular elections.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In his first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a>, in 290 BCE, his colleague was P. Cornelius Rufinus, an ancestor of the dictator Lucius Cornelius Sulla Felix. Dentatus defeated both the Samnites and the Sabines and was granted two triumphs. On his return he undertook a large public work, partly draining Lake Velinus. In 283 BCE he took the place of Lucius Caecilius Metellus Denter, who had been killed at the Battle of Arretium, as praetor or possibly consul, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> says he drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> from their territory, opening the way for a Roman colony at Sena.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">Consul</a> again in 275 BCE, he fought Pyrrhus at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Beneventum</a>, after which Pyrrhus left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. He held a further consulship the next year, defeated the Lucani and was granted an ovation. He served as censor in 272 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 270 BCE Dentatus and Lucius Papirius Cursor were chosen as commissioners for the Anio Vetus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s second aqueduct, which he paid for with his own share of the booty from his campaigns. He died while it was being built, and his fellow commissioner Marcus Fulvius Flaccus completed it.</p><p>He was remembered as frugal and incorruptible. In the best-known story, Samnite envoys bringing costly gifts found him by his hearth roasting turnips; he refused the gifts, saying he preferred ruling those who possessed gold to possessing it himself. The story may be an invention of Cato, but it inspired paintings by Jacopo Amigoni, Govert Flinck and others.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manius Curius Dentatus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Manius Curius Dentatus</b> conquered the Sabines in 290 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/sabines">Sabines</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ancient Rome, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-280" aria-label="Ancient Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Manius Curius Dentatus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-280">What was here in 280 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
